# {{.Name}} ({{.Type}})

Provides an LDAP entry. This can be used to create, modify, and delete LDAP entries.

Two special handlings are implemented (somehow in deviation from the generic approach in this provider):

The read function for the resource uses
[NewSearchRequest](https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/go-ldap/ldap/v3@v3.4.5#NewSearchRequest) to read the entry 
and thus a filter has to be specified.
Therefor `(objectClass=*)` is used as a filter not filtering anything 
(each entry has an objectClass as specified in [RFC 4512 Section 3.3.](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4512.html#section-3.3)).

The read function for the resource ignores the attribute corresponding to the relative part (RDN) of the DN (https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc4512.html#section-2.3).
This is necessary because this part is added as attribute to the LDAP entry by the LDAP server but is not specified in the terraform code.
By now this only supports single valued RDNs (s. https://github.com/l-with/terraform-provider-ldap/issues/34). 
In the example below the LDAP attribute `uid` is ignored on read because `uid=jimmit01` is the RDN 
and the attribute `uid` with the value `jimmit01` is added implicitely by the LDAP server (although not part of `data_json`).

## Ignored Attributes

Attributes listed in `ignore_attributes` or matching `ignore_attribute_patterns` are neither read from nor written to LDAP, not even on Create.
Values for ignored attributes in `data_json` therefore have no effect: they are not written, do not cause a perpetual diff, and a warning is issued.
Use `data_json_create_defaults` to set an attribute only on creation and ignore it afterwards.

## Per Value Updates

By default an Update replaces all values of a changed attribute with the values in `data_json`.
For attributes listed in `per_value_update_attributes` only the values removed from `data_json` are deleted and only the values added to `data_json` are added.
The result is the same, but values added outside of Terraform after the plan (e.g. members added to a group concurrently) are not deleted by the apply, since the plan did not show their removal.
The next plan shows their removal, if they are not in `data_json`.
Before the update the current values are read, so that a value added or deleted concurrently outside of Terraform, which the update would add or delete as well, does not let the update fail.

Values of these attributes are compared case-insensitively, like the LDAP server compares e.g. DNs:
OpenLDAP returns DNs in member values with lower case attribute types (`uid=jimmit01,ou=users,...`),
Active Directory with upper case attribute types (`CN=Jim Mit,OU=users,...`).
A value in `data_json` differing only in case therefore neither causes a diff nor an update.

```terraform
resource "ldap_entry" "group" {
  dn = "cn=group,ou=groups,dc=example,dc=com"
  per_value_update_attributes = [
    "member",
  ]
  data_json = jsonencode({
    objectClass = ["groupOfNames"]
    member      = ["uid=jimmit01,ou=users,dc=example,dc=com"]
  })
}
```

